export const lpContent={
 'doctor':{
  title:'Doctor',one:'How bodies get checked and healed — and how to be brave at a check-up.',
  seoTitle:'Doctor Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach What Doctors Do (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach what a doctor does, point out the picture together, play gentle doctor, and prepare your child for a calm, honest check-up.',
  intro:'This lesson shows your child what doctors actually do, what their tools are for, and how a check-up works — so the real thing feels known instead of scary.',
  what:'A doctor is a body helper. When someone is sick or hurt, the doctor checks, finds out why, and treats it.',
  mean:'Doctors work in clinics, hospitals and health vans. Their tools each have a job: a stethoscope listens to heartbeats, a small torch lights up ears and throats, a bandage protects a healing cut.',
  know:'Your child needs to know three things: doctors help bodies get better; a check-up is looking and listening, one small step at a time; and telling a grown-up where it hurts is the fastest way to get help.',
  why:'Doctor play and doctor talk give your child the words and the script for real appointments. A known story is a calmer story — for both of you.',
  dev:'Vocabulary (doctor, patient, stethoscope, check-up), body awareness, asking-for-help skills, and trust in helpers — plus sequenced thinking as your child retells the visit in order.',
  vocab:'doctor, patient, stethoscope, check-up, healthy, medicine',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a doctor. Doctors help our bodies when we are sick or hurt."\n2) Point to the picture: find the stethoscope, the coat, the kind face. Ask what each might be for.\n3) Explain the visit in order: we say what hurts, the doctor looks and listens, then we get a plan to feel better.\n4) Be honest: some checks feel odd — a cold stethoscope, a puff of air, a look up your nose. Saying how it feels to the doctor is always allowed.\n5) Practise the visit end to end in pretend play before any real appointment.',
  say:'"When something hurts, you tell me and we visit the doctor. The doctor looks and listens very carefully, and then your body gets a plan to get better. You can always tell the doctor how it feels."',
  notice:'Ask your child to find: what is on the doctor’s head or neck? What is in the doctor’s hands? Where do you think the doctor is working? Who is the doctor helping?',
  ask:'"What does a doctor do all day?" "Which tool listens to your heart?" "What could you tell the doctor if your tummy hurt?" "How could we make a check-up feel easier?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The doctor helps me get better," "The stethoscope goes thump-thump on my chest," or practise the brave sentence: "My knee hurts here."',
  real:'Point out doctor things in daily life: the clinic you pass, the nurse at preschool, the first-aid box at the pool, medicine with a "grown-ups only" label at the pharmacy.',
  play:'Teddy clinic: your child is the doctor. Listen to Ted’s heart with a cardboard-tube stethoscope, look in his ears with a torch (off), wrap a tissue bandage, and say kindly: "Tell me where it hurts." Swap roles so your child is the patient too — that is the bravery rehearsal.',
  tips:'Never use the doctor as a threat or a punishment — it turns a helper into a bogeyman. Read a doctor-visit book the day before appointments, let your child pack a toy, and afterwards name the brave moments out loud.',
  seeAlso:'What happens at the doctor? Take a gentle visit: the <a href="/extra-classes/doctor-visit/">Doctor Visit class</a> walks a whole check-up in order — appointment to goodbye — with a teddy bear on every step.',
  check:'Ask: "Who do you tell when something hurts? What does the doctor listen with? What could you say to the doctor at your check-up?" Three answers = lesson landed.',
  safety:'Medicine is only given by grown-ups — never take pills or syrup like sweets, even when it smells nice. Sharp things at the clinic are the doctor’s tools only.'},
 'nurse':{
  title:'Nurse',one:'The caring helpers who check, comfort and explain — and how to care for someone too.',
  seoTitle:'Nurse Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach What Nurses Do (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what nurses do beside the doctor, their everyday tools, a caring-corner role play and gentle check-up language.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches who nurses are, what they do all day beside doctors, and gives your child a caring job to practise at home.',
  what:'A nurse is a caring helper who looks after sick and hurt people — checking them, comforting them and explaining everything.',
  mean:'Nurses work in hospitals, clinics, schools and vaccination vans. They measure temperature, wrap bandages, give the medicine doctors prescribe, and teach families how to keep healing at home.',
  know:'Nurses stay with patients the longest — they are the ones who explain, comfort and check again. When your child knows the nurse by role, check-ups gain a familiar friendly face.',
  why:'Nursing is visible kindness: calm voice, gentle hands, careful checking. Children absorb that caring is something people DO — and copy it on dolls, siblings and pets.',
  dev:'Empathy, gentle-touch control, sequencing (check → comfort → explain), health vocabulary, and the confidence to ask "what are you doing now?" during real care.',
  vocab:'nurse, patient, thermometer, bandage, comfort, gentle',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a nurse. Nurses take care of people — with the doctor and on their own."\n2) Point out the picture: what is the nurse holding? What is on the walls? Who is being cared for?\n3) Walk the nurse’s day: check temperature → wrap the arm → explain each step → smile. Act it with your hands as you say it.\n4) Teach the caring script: "I am going to check you now, all right? Tell me if it feels strange."\n5) Practise on a patient who cannot talk — a teddy — so all attention goes to the gentle hands.',
  say:'"Nurses help the doctor take care of you. They check how you are, they make hurts feel better, and they explain every single thing they do. Nurses have gentle hands and kind voices."',
  notice:'Ask your child: what is the nurse doing first? What is on the tray or in the pockets? Can you find a smile — on the nurse or the patient? What tells you this nurse is being careful?',
  ask:'"What does a nurse check first?" "What would you say before you wrap a teddy’s paw?" "Who takes care of you when you are poorly?" "How can our hands be nurse-hands?"',
  childSay:'Practise the caring lines out loud: "I will check you now — tell me if it feels strange," "All wrapped up, all better," and the asking line: "What are you doing now?"',
  real:'Nurses are everywhere once you look: the school first-aid drawer, the clinic waiting room, the vaccination nurse, bandage boxes at home. Each spotting moment is a mini-recap.',
  play:'Caring corner: one blanket "bed", one doll or teddy, toilet-paper bandages, a paper "thermometer". Your child is the nurse — check, wrap, explain, comfort. Ten minutes of this is a whole social-emotional lesson.',
  tips:'Praise the caring behaviours you see ("you checked Ted so gently") — nurse talk makes gentleness cool. If a real check-up is coming, tell your child the nurse will explain first; knowing that halves the wobbles.',
  check:'Ask: "Who checks people beside the doctor? What do nurses wrap hurts with? Show me your gentle nurse hands." The hands will tell you everything.',
  safety:'Bandage play is pretend — real hurts need a grown-up’s eyes first. Medicine, even "just once more", is a grown-up job only.'},
 'paramedic':{
  title:'Paramedic',one:'The fast helpers in the loud ambulance — what really happens in an emergency.',
  seoTitle:'Paramedic Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach What Paramedics Do (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what paramedics do, why sirens are loud, practising your emergency number and a calm teddy-rescue game.',
  intro:'This lesson turns siren panic into understanding: who is inside the ambulance, what they carry, and what your family does in a real emergency.',
  what:'A paramedic is a fast health helper. In a big emergency, they come quickly in an ambulance to help the person and take them to hospital.',
  mean:'An ambulance is a rescue car with lights, a siren and a mini-hospital inside: stretchers, oxygen, bandages, a radio. Paramedics check the patient on the spot and keep helping all the ride to hospital.',
  know:'Three things your child should carry home: the siren means help is on the way, the emergency number (practise yours), and in an emergency we find a grown-up — fast and loudly.',
  why:'Emergencies are frightening because they are unknown. Naming the helpers, the vehicle and the family plan gives your child a script — and scripts shrink fear.',
  dev:'Emergency vocabulary, number memory, listening under stress (practised calmly!), community-trust building, and sequencing: call → helpers arrive → check → hospital.',
  vocab:'paramedic, ambulance, emergency, siren, stretcher, first aid',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a paramedic. When there is a big emergency, paramedics come fast to help."\n2) Find the ambulance parts: lights, siren, the cross, the wheels that go very fast.\n3) Explain the noise honestly: "The siren is loud so cars hear it and move — that is how help gets there quickly."\n4) Learn your family emergency number today: say it, tap it on a toy phone, sing it. Three tiny rehearsals beat one big lecture.\n5) Rehearse the family rule: big trouble → shout for a grown-up → grown-up calls. Never play-call emergencies for fun.',
  say:'"If something bad happens, we tell a grown-up right away — loud and clear. Grown-ups call the emergency number, and the paramedics come fast with their ambulance to help."',
  notice:'Ask: what is on top of the ambulance? What colours do you see? What is the paramedic holding? Where do you think the patient rides — can you find the windows?',
  ask:'"Who comes in an ambulance?" "Why is the siren so loud?" "What would you do if someone fell and could not get up?" "What is our emergency number?"',
  childSay:'Practise the shout: "Grown-up, come quick, help please!" and the number, spoken clearly twice. Then the calm sentence: "The paramedics are coming to help."',
  real:'When a siren passes, stop and listen: "Someone needs help — and helpers are coming." Ambulance open days let children sit inside; the radio alone is worth the trip.',
  play:'Teddy rescue: Ted "falls" off the sofa. Your child shouts for a grown-up (you), you "call", the toy ambulance rushes over, paramedic checks Ted on a shoebox stretcher, everyone recovers. Repeat weekly — rehearsal is the point.',
  tips:'Sensitive children may need the siren named BEFORE it happens. Keep the number learning light — songs and toy phones, never quizzes. Praise every "I told a grown-up", real or pretend.',
  check:'Ask: "Who comes to help in a big emergency? What do we do first? Say our emergency number." Loud-and-clear shout + number = pass.',
  safety:'Emergency numbers are for real emergencies only — never for fun calls. Ambulance bays and moving trucks are out of bounds; children watch from far back with a grown-up.'},
 'police-officer':{
  title:'Police Officer',one:'The neighborhood’s safety helpers — the badge, the helping and the get-found plan.',
  seoTitle:'Police Officer Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach What Officers Do (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what police officers do, how the badge helps a lost child, and a calm get-found rehearsal for families.',
  intro:'This lesson introduces police officers as neighborhood helpers and reheases the one plan every child should own: what to do if you ever get lost.',
  what:'A police officer is a safety helper for everyone in the neighborhood — the person to find when someone is lost, scared or in trouble.',
  mean:'Officers patrol on foot, on bikes and in marked cars. They help lost children, stop dangerous driving, sort out arguments and find out what happened after accidents. A badge and a radio are their tools.',
  know:'Two messages, in balance: officers are helpers we can trust — AND in any worry, our first job is to find a grown-up we know. Practise both; they work together.',
  why:'"Find the badge" is a concrete, findable plan for a small person in a big scary moment. Concrete beats abstract at four.',
  dev:'Community trust, safety scripting, self-talk under stress ("I am lost, I will find a helper"), plus personal-information memory: your name, your grown-up’s name, your street.',
  vocab:'police officer, badge, safe, lost, trust, radio',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a police officer. Officers keep our neighborhood safe and help anyone in trouble."\n2) Find the badge and the radio. "The badge tells everyone: this is a helper. The radio talks to other helpers."\n3) Tell one helping story concretely: an officer found a lost child and walked them back to their grown-up.\n4) Learn the get-found plan today: stay where you are → look for a grown-up with a badge or a parent with children → say "I am lost" + your grown-up’s name.\n5) Rehearse it as a game with teddies — then quietly again next week.',
  say:'"Police officers are helpers. If we are ever lost, we stay where we are and ask a grown-up with a badge: ‘I am lost — my grown-up is…’ And we always tell someone we trust, too."',
  notice:'Ask: can you find the badge? The radio? What is the officer wearing? Does the officer look kind? What might the officer be looking at?',
  ask:'"Who do officers help?" "What would you say if you were lost?" "What does the badge mean?" "Who are the grown-ups YOU trust?"',
  childSay:'The key sentence, spoken twice, loud and clear: "I am lost — please help me find my grown-up." Plus your family’s grown-up name and street, practised until easy.',
  real:'Wave to officers you pass; visit a station open day. Toy police cars in the bath become helping stories when you narrate who they rescue.',
  play:'Teddy is lost in the "shop" (another room). Your child the officer finds him, kneels down, and teddy practises the sentence. Swap: your child is lost, you are the officer. Repetition is the safety net.',
  tips:'Keep it warm, never scary: officers are helpers, not monsters and not magicians. Balance matters — "we look for a known grown-up first, and a badge is a good find". Avoid threat-based policing talk entirely.',
  check:'Ask: "What does a police officer do? What would you say if you were lost? What is my first name?" Clear sentence + your name = lesson landed.',
  safety:'Rehearse calmly, never with threats. Agree the family rule together and repeat it on every busy-street outing: stay put, find a helper, say the sentence.'},
 'firefighter':{
  title:'Firefighter',one:'The fire helpers and their big truck — plus the escape plan every family practises.',
  seoTitle:'Firefighter Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Fire Safety (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what firefighters do, why the gear looks scary, matches-are-grown-up-tools, and a family escape-plan game.',
  intro:'This lesson covers what firefighters do — fighting fires AND preventing them — and walks your family through the one drill that matters.',
  what:'A firefighter is an emergency helper who puts out fires, rescues people and pets, and teaches everyone how to stop fires from starting.',
  mean:'Firefighters ride a big red truck with ladders, hoses and water. Their gear — helmet, heavy coat, gloves, an air mask — protects them inside smoke and heat.',
  know:'The three fire rules for small people: matches and lighters are grown-up tools (tell a grown-up if you find one); smoke means get low, get out, stay out; firefighters in masks are helpers, even when they look and sound strange.',
  why:'Fire is the scariest "what if" of early childhood — and the most fixable. A drilled plan plus mask-familiarity changes outcomes; this is the highest-value safety lesson in the whole class.',
  dev:'Safety scripting, family teamwork, sequencing (alarm → low → out → meeting spot), mask-and-noise desensitisation, and the prevention mindset: firefighters stop fires before they start.',
  vocab:'firefighter, smoke, helmet, escape, meeting spot, emergency',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a firefighter. Firefighters put out fires and rescue people."\n2) Find the gear: helmet, coat, boots. "The suit is thick and the mask looks funny — but it is a helper suit. It keeps the firefighter safe inside smoke."\n3) Talk prevention first: "Most firefighter work is stopping fires before they happen. Matches? Grown-up tools. Candle? Grown-up tool."\n4) Teach the escape script: hear the alarm → get low and crawl under the smoke → get outside → STAY out → meet at our family spot.\n5) Drill it as a game today: crawl between sofa cushions, count to the meeting spot, cheer. Repeat twice a year.',
  say:'"If there is ever smoke or fire, we do not hide — we get low, we get outside, and we stay outside at our meeting spot. The firefighters come to help us. We never go back inside, not even for a toy."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the firefighter wearing? Can you find the helmet — why is it hard? What colour is the truck behind? What does the firefighter carry?',
  ask:'"What do firefighters ride in?" "What do we do if we hear the smoke alarm?" "Are matches for children or grown-ups?" "Where is our family meeting spot?"',
  childSay:'The escape line, shouted happily: "Get low, get out, stay out!" plus "Matches are grown-up tools — I will tell you!" And your address, spoken slowly once.',
  real:'Walk past the fire station and look at the truck doors. Point out smoke alarms at home and press-test them together so the sound is known, not feared.',
  play:'Fire-drill game: teddy "cooking" leaves a "fire" (red scarf). Alarm sounds (clap-clap), everyone crawls low to the door, out to the meeting spot (the hallway plant). Then — the firefighter puppet arrives to put it out. Repeat until boring; boring is the goal.',
  tips:'Masks scare children who have never seen one. Fire-station open days fix this permanently — the voice behind the mask is a person who loves dogs. Never use fire or firefighters as a threat.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we do when the alarm sounds? What do we do if we find matches? Where do we meet outside?" Three crisp answers = drill landed.',
  safety:'This is the safety lesson: matches, lighters and candles are grown-up tools — always tell a grown-up. Real drill: low, out, stay out, never go back in. Do the drill, don’t just read it.'},
 'crossing-guard':{
  title:'Crossing Guard',one:'Stop, look, listen, hold hands — the crossing routine that walks to school with you.',
  seoTitle:'Crossing Guard Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Road Safety (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what crossing guards do, the stop-look-listen routine and a hold-a-grown-up’s-hand practice for every street crossing.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the crossing guard’s job and drills the road-crossing routine your child will use every single day of childhood.',
  what:'A crossing guard is a road helper who stops traffic so children and families can cross the street safely.',
  mean:'Crossing guards wear bright vests and carry a stop sign. They stand at busy crossings at school times, watch the cars, step out, hold up the sign, and wave people across when the way is clear.',
  know:'The routine: wait at the kerb, listen for the guard, cross only on the guard’s or your grown-up’s signal, and hold hands the whole way. Streets are for crossing, never for playing.',
  why:'Roads are the most dangerous place in a child’s daily life. A drill with a rhythm — stop, look, listen, hold — is remembered by the body, not just the head.',
  dev:'Self-control at kerbs (the hardest skill!), left-right scanning, listening for engines, hand-holding cooperation, and traffic vocabulary.',
  vocab:'crossing guard, kerb, traffic, stop sign, safe, cross',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a crossing guard. They stop the cars so we can cross safely."\n2) Find the stop sign and the bright vest. "Cars can see the bright colours from far away."\n3) Teach the four beats with hand motions: STOP (hand flat), LOOK (hand over eyes), LISTEN (hand to ear), HOLD (squeeze hands).\n4) Practise the beats at a pretend kerb — a rug edge — with teddies as traffic.\n5) Then do it for real on your next walk, narrating quietly: "Stop… look… listen… now we hold hands and cross."',
  say:'"At the kerb we stop. We look left, look right, look left again. We listen for cars. Then we hold my hand and cross together — and we never run."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the guard holding up? What colours can you find on the vest? Which way is the guard looking? Where are the children standing — on the road or at the kerb?',
  ask:'"What does the stop sign tell the cars?" "What do we do first at the kerb?" "Why do we hold hands?" "Can we play on the road?"',
  childSay:'The four beats, chanted: "Stop, look, listen, hold!" And the rule, in their own words: "We cross with a grown-up, never alone."',
  real:'Greet your real crossing guard by role — "there is the crossing guard keeping us safe" — and let your child wave. The routine becomes a relationship, and relationships stick.',
  play:'Cushion kerb: line up cushions as the road, teddies drive toy cars, your child is the guard with a paper stop sign. They must hold the sign, count "one, two, three, safe!", then wave the teddies across. Swap roles.',
  tips:'Never step into the road with a moving car "to show what happens" — the drill is always done safe. Reward the wait: patience at the kerb is the win, not speed.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we do first at the kerb? Who stops the cars? Do we run or walk?" Stop + guard + walking = pass.',
  safety:'Never cross alone, never run across, never play near roads — even quiet ones. Cars need a long time to stop; small children are hard for drivers to see.'},
 'lifeguard':{
  title:'Lifeguard',one:'The watchful helper at the water — whistle rules and safe swimming habits.',
  seoTitle:'Lifeguard Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Water Safety (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what lifeguards do, what the whistle means, walk-don’t-run rules and a bath-time freeze game.',
  intro:'This lesson introduces the lifeguard’s watching job and sets the water rules your child will use at every pool, beach and bath.',
  what:'A lifeguard is a water-safety helper who watches everyone in the water and helps the moment something goes wrong.',
  mean:'Lifeguards sit in a tall chair by pools or on beaches, watching all the time. They blow a whistle when something is unsafe, rescue people with a buoy or tube, and know first aid.',
  know:'Water rules for fours: we ask a grown-up before any water play; a lifeguard’s whistle means freeze and listen; we walk near water, never run; a grown-up is always in the water with non-swimmers.',
  why:'Rules feel unfair until they have a person and a reason. The lifeguard — watching IS helping — makes pool rules make sense, which is exactly when children follow them.',
  dev:'Listening-and-freezing control (a genuine safety skill), rule reasoning, water vocabulary, and the asking-before-doing habit that generalises to every risky place.',
  vocab:'lifeguard, whistle, rescue, deep, shallow, walk',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a lifeguard. They watch the water and keep swimmers safe."\n2) Find the tall chair, the whistle, the rescue ring. "Sitting up high lets the lifeguard see EVERYONE."\n3) Give the whistle meaning: "One whistle means stop and look at the lifeguard. It is not scolding — it is helping."\n4) Teach the asking habit: any water — pool, bucket, bath, sea — gets a grown-up check first.\n5) Rehearse the freeze game at bath time so the listening reflex exists before a real pool day.',
  say:'"The lifeguard watches the water all the time. When we hear the whistle, we stop and listen. Near water we walk — and a grown-up is always with us."',
  notice:'Ask: where does the lifeguard sit — high or low? Why so high? What is the lifeguard holding? Can you find someone swimming? What colours say "safety" on this card?',
  ask:'"What does the lifeguard’s whistle mean?" "Why does the lifeguard sit so high?" "Do we run or walk near the pool?" "Who do we ask before we go in the water?"',
  childSay:'Practise the freeze: you whistle softly, your child stops and looks. And the asking line: "May I go in the water?" — said before any water play.',
  real:'At the pool, find the lifeguard together: "See how they never stop watching? That is their whole job." Beach flags and pool-depth signs are lifeguard language — decode one.',
  play:'Bath lifeguard: your child is the lifeguard on the "chair" (closed toilet lid, towels for a tower). You wash a doll; lifeguard blows a pretend whistle; doll freezes; lifeguard says "safe!" Swap and let them run the whistle show.',
  tips:'Never treat the whistle as punishment — it is a helper’s voice. Praise fast freezing; the speed IS the safety. If your child is swim-shy, the lifeguard can be introduced as "the person who helps everyone feel brave".',
  check:'Ask: "What does the whistle mean? Walk or run at the pool? Who goes in the water with you?" Freeze + walk + grown-up = pass.',
  safety:'Never swim without a grown-up actually in the water. No running on wet pool sides. Ponds, buckets and paddling pools count as water — the asking rule covers them all.'},
 'teacher':{
  title:'Teacher',one:'Inside the classroom — how teachers help us learn, and a play-school rehearsal.',
  seoTitle:'Teacher Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach What Teachers Do (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what teachers do all day, raising hands and asking questions, and a play-school game that builds school confidence.',
  intro:'This lesson opens the classroom door: what teachers actually do, the little rules that make class work, and a rehearsal for your child’s own school days.',
  what:'A teacher is a learning helper who shows children new things — letters, numbers, songs, science, and how to be a good friend.',
  mean:'Teachers work in schools. They plan the day, explain ideas step by step, read aloud, help with sharing and turn-taking, and tell families how the day went.',
  know:'Classroom know-how: teachers love questions, we raise a hand to take a turn, and "I need help" is a welcome sentence, not a failure.',
  why:'A teacher is your child’s first grown-up outside the family. Knowing the job and the routines turns first-days wobble into "I know how this works".',
  dev:'School-readiness scripting, question-asking courage, listening turns, memory retell (what did we learn today?), and respect-for-guides that transfers to coaches and librarians.',
  vocab:'teacher, classroom, raise your hand, learn, question, turn',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a teacher. Teachers help children learn new things every day."\n2) Find the classroom things on the card: books, board, children listening. What is YOUR classroom like?\n3) Explain the rhythm of a school day: come in, listen, try, play, ask, go home. Use your hands to show the day’s shape.\n4) Teach the two golden moves: raise your hand to speak, and say "I need help" proudly.\n5) Rehearse with play-school so the moves feel familiar before they are needed.',
  say:'"Teachers help us learn. When you wonder about something, raise your hand and ask — teachers LOVE questions. And if something is tricky, saying ‘help me please’ is exactly right."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the teacher holding or pointing at? What are the children doing — are they taking turns? What can you see on the walls? Does the classroom look calm or loud?',
  ask:'"What does a teacher do all day?" "What do we do when we want to say something in class?" "What could you ask your teacher tomorrow?"',
  childSay:'Practise the two moves: "Can I say something?" (hand up) and "I need help, please." Both said proudly — needing help is a skill, not a shame.',
  real:'At home, teachers are everywhere: swimming coaches, library story readers, dance teachers. Name the role each time — "your coach is teaching like a teacher".',
  play:'Play-school: your child teaches teddies one song, one letter and one rule. Then swap — you are the student who raises a hand, asks a silly-good question and says "I need help". The swap is where the magic lands.',
  tips:'Never joke "wait till real school!" — school should sound like a place with friendly helpers. Ask "what did your teacher show today?" at dinner; retelling builds memory and gives you a window.',
  check:'Ask: "What does a teacher do? What do we do when we want to talk in class? What do we say when something is tricky?" Question + hand + help = pass.'},
 'librarian':{
  title:'Librarian',one:'Inside the library — how to find books, borrow them and bring them back.',
  seoTitle:'Librarian Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Library Skills (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what librarians do, how borrowing works, quiet-voice skills and a first library visit plan.',
  intro:'This lesson makes the library a known place before the first visit: who the librarian is, how borrowing works, and the little skills that make trips joyful.',
  what:'A librarian is a book helper who takes care of the library and helps every visitor find exactly the right book.',
  mean:'Libraries hold thousands of books everyone can borrow for free. Librarians arrange them so they can be found, run story times, and lend books with a library card.',
  know:'Library skills: books live in order, we ask the librarian to find things, borrowing means take-home-and-bring-back, and the library voice is soft.',
  why:'A library card is a child’s first membership of the grown-up world. Knowing the borrowing loop — choose, borrow, care, return — is responsibility with stories as the reward.',
  dev:'Sequencing (choose→borrow→read→return), care-of-shared-things, asking-adults-for-help, quiet-voice self-regulation, and print motivation — the strongest early-reading predictor.',
  vocab:'librarian, library, borrow, return, story time, card',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a librarian. They help us find books and take them home."\n2) Find the shelves, the books, the helper. "There are so many books — the librarian knows where every one lives."\n3) Explain borrowing like a tick-tock: choose a book → the librarian stamps it → we take it home → we read it lots → we bring it back so the next child can love it.\n4) Practise the library voice: whisper "where are the truck books?" — then practise asking it, because librarians LOVE that question.\n5) Plan the first visit: what two books will you look for?',
  say:'"The librarian helps us find wonderful books. We borrow them with our card, read them at home, and bring them back for the next family. Librarians know where every dinosaur hides."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the librarian doing — reading, helping, tidying? How many books can you count? What would YOU look for first on those shelves?',
  ask:'"Who helps us find books?" "What happens when we borrow a book?" "What do we do with a book we love and finished?" "What should we ask the librarian on our visit?"',
  childSay:'Practise the ask: "Where are the books about trucks, please?" And the borrowing line at the counter: "I would like to borrow these books, please."',
  real:'Libraries run free story times — go once and your child meets the librarian in their favourite role. Spot "return here" boxes around town: book journeys never stop.',
  play:'Home library: line up ten books face-out, make paper "cards", take turns being librarian and borrower. The stamp can be a sticker; the return basket is the book-drop. Repeat until the loop is automatic.',
  tips:'Let your child carry the card and hand the books over — the transaction IS the lesson. Damaged-book moments happen; talk kindly about caring for borrowed things and move on.',
  check:'Ask: "Who helps us in the library? What do we do with a borrowed book when we finish? Show me your library voice." Helper + return + whisper = pass.'},
 'mail-carrier':{
  title:'Mail Carrier',one:'The journey of a letter — from your post box to a grandparent’s hands.',
  seoTitle:'Mail Carrier Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach the Journey of Mail (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what mail carriers do, how a letter travels, address and stamp basics, and a post-a-picture activity.',
  intro:'This lesson follows one letter’s journey and gives your child a real one to send — mail is the most concrete community helper magic there is.',
  what:'A mail carrier is a delivery helper who brings letters, cards and parcels to every home on the street — and carries our post away to the people we love.',
  mean:'Mail carriers walk or drive a route with a big bag, sorted in address order. Letters travel from post box to sorting office to route — sometimes with a plane or train in between.',
  know:'The posting loop: write → envelope → stamp → post box → mail carrier → the right letterbox. And the golden rule: our letterbox and mail truck are the carrier’s workplace, not toys.',
  why:'Posting real mail connects writing to love: your scribble becomes a grandparent’s smile. Address, stamp and delivery stop being abstract words and become a thing your child DID.',
  dev:'Sequencing across days (write today, arrive Thursday — patience!), address awareness, name-writing motivation, community maps, and giver’s joy.',
  vocab:'mail carrier, letter, envelope, stamp, post box, deliver',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a mail carrier. They bring letters to our home and take ours away."\n2) Find the bag, the letters, the truck. "The bag is heavy — the letters inside are for many different houses!"\n3) Trace the journey with your finger: our table → post box → sorting office (the letters get sorted like socks!) → the mail carrier’s bag → Grandmother’s letterbox.\n4) Read an envelope together: name, street, town. "The address is the letter’s home address!"\n5) Make a real letter today — the lesson only lands when something is actually posted.',
  say:'"The mail carrier brings us letters and parcels, and takes ours away to the people we love. A drawing from your hands can travel across the whole town — or the whole world."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the mail carrier carrying? How many letterboxes can you count? What colour is the truck? Is it raining or sunny — do mail carriers still work?',
  ask:'"Who brings our letters? Where does our letter go after the post box? What is on the front of an envelope? Who could we send a picture to?"',
  childSay:'Practise the send-off: "Dear Grandma, I made this for you!" — your child "writes" their name and tells you whose letter it is and where it is going.',
  real:'Walk to a real post box and post something real. Track bin-and-mail days on a family calendar; watch for the carrier on your street and wave.',
  play:'Home post office: shoebox counter, paper stamps, envelopes, a sock "mail bag". Family members write notes; your child sorts by person and delivers to letterboxes made of cereal boxes. Sorting = the real job.',
  tips:'The waiting is the lesson: mark on the calendar when the letter should arrive. If the carrier comes, a wave and "thank you!" from the doorstep beats any worksheet.',
  check:'Ask: "Who brings our mail? Where does a letter go after we post it? What do we put on the envelope?" Carrier + sorting + address = pass.',
  safety:'Never open other people’s post or mailboxes, and never play behind a parked delivery van — drivers cannot see small people there.'},
 'bus-driver':{
  title:'Bus Driver',one:'Stop by stop, safely — how bus rides work and your child’s first fare.',
  seoTitle:'Bus Driver Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Bus Rides (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what bus drivers do, how riding works step by step, seating safety and a ride-one-stop adventure.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the whole bus experience — waiting, boarding, riding, ringing, exiting — so the first real ride belongs to your child.',
  what:'A bus driver is a travel helper who drives people around the neighborhood, stop by stop, safely.',
  mean:'Buses run on fixed routes with marked stops. The driver steers, opens the doors, keeps everyone safe and follows the route plan. Passengers pay, sit and hold on.',
  know:'Bus know-how: wait at the stop, board after others leave, pay or tap, sit and hold, ring for your stop, wait for full stop before standing, and exit with a grown-up.',
  why:'Public transport is a bundle of life skills in one ride: patience, money, seating, sequencing. And counting stops is real maths with motion.',
  dev:'Sequencing and waiting, money handling, stop counting (maths!), bell-pressing agency, and road-safety transfer: buses are big, drivers sit high, small people are hard to see.',
  vocab:'bus driver, bus stop, fare, route, stop bell, board',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a bus driver. They take people where they need to go — stop by stop."\n2) Find the wheel, the doors, the seats. "The driver watches the road with big mirrors so everyone rides safely."\n3) Walk the ride sequence with your hands: stop → doors open → pay → sit and hold → ring → doors open → off.\n4) Practise the hold: sitting bottoms stay on seats, hands hold the rail, and we wait for the bus to stop fully before standing.\n5) Plan a real one-stop ride this week — one stop out, walk back, memory made.',
  say:'"The bus driver takes us safely to our stop. We wait for the bus to stop, pay our fare, sit down and hold on. When our stop comes, we ring the bell!"',
  notice:'Ask: where does the driver sit? Can you find the doors? How many passengers can you count? What is outside the windows?',
  ask:'"Who drives the bus? What do we do when we get on? When can we stand up? What do we press before our stop?"',
  childSay:'Practise the fare hand-over: "One ticket, please!" And the safety rule in their words: "I stay seated and hold on until the bus stops."',
  real:'Any bus ride becomes a lesson: count stops aloud, spot the driver’s mirrors, find the route map. Trams, trains and ferries all run the same script.',
  play:'Chair bus: line up chairs, punch tickets from scrap paper, call stops ("Next stop: Library!"), a spoon in a cup rings the bell. Everyone boards with teddies and pays with buttons.',
  tips:'Board after passengers get off; let your child drop coins in the fare box — the clink is their moment. Talk to the driver when boarding if the queue allows; "thank you, driver!" lands beautifully.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we do at the stop? When do we stand up? What do we press before our stop?" Wait + sit-till-stop + bell = pass.',
  safety:'Stay seated and holding while the bus moves. Never walk directly behind a bus — the driver cannot see small people there. Always exit with a grown-up.'},
 'pilot':{
  title:'Pilot',one:'Up in the cockpit — how flights happen, from check to take-off to landing.',
  seoTitle:'Pilot Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach What Pilots Do (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what pilots do before and during flights, the tower’s role, airport safety and a chair-plane take-off game.',
  intro:'This lesson walks a flight from checklist to landing and turns airport overwhelm into a known story with your child as co-pilot.',
  what:'A pilot is a flying helper who flies aeroplanes, taking people and things safely across the sky.',
  mean:'Pilots sit in the cockpit with screens, buttons and a steering wheel called a yoke. Before every flight they check the plane, plan the route and talk with the airport tower by radio.',
  know:'Flight know-how: pilots check everything BEFORE flying, the tower helps guide them, and at the airport children hold a grown-up’s hand and stay behind the lines.',
  why:'Airports are sensory chaos for fours — belts, queues, loudspeakers. A pilot story gives the trip a hero and a script, and contrails turn every future sky-gaze into spotting helpers.',
  dev:'Sequencing (check→taxi→take-off→fly→land), big-map thinking (world is huge, planes cross it), radio-play dialogue, weather curiosity, and waiting skills at real airports.',
  vocab:'pilot, cockpit, tower, take off, land, co-pilot',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a pilot. Pilots fly aeroplanes and keep everyone on board safe."\n2) Find the cockpit, the windows, the wings. "The pilot sits at the very front — the best office window in the world!"\n3) Tell the flight in five beats: check the plane → taxi down the runway → take off → fly above the clouds → land. Act each with your arm.\n4) Explain the tower: "The people in the tower watch every plane and talk to pilots by radio — helper talking to helper."\n5) Play the whole flight before any real trip, so belts and take-off sounds are old friends.',
  say:'"The pilot checks everything, then flies the plane up above the clouds. The tower talks to the pilot on the radio the whole way. Before we take off, everyone buckles up — even teddies."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the pilot wearing? What can you see through the cockpit windows? Where do the wings go? What might the pilot’s hands be doing?',
  ask:'"Who flies the plane? Who talks to the pilot on the radio? What happens right before take-off? What is above the clouds?"',
  childSay:'The countdown: "Three, two, one — take off!" And the pre-flight line: "We check everything first." Plus the airport rule: "Hold a grown-up’s hand."',
  real:'Watch real contrails and wave. Airport trips: belts go in the tray, planes go up. Picture books about first flights read the night before work wonders.',
  play:'Chair plane: two chairs, seatbelts of dressing-gown belts, teddies stowed. Pilot announces: "Cabin ready? Check the wings!" Tower (you, holding a toy phone) clears take-off; everyone counts down; arms out; fly over the ocean rug; land softly at the sofa airport. Swap tower and pilot roles.',
  tips:'Loud take-off noises are less scary when named in advance: "The engines roar when we go fast — that is the plane getting strong." Let your child pack a plane bag: agency calms nerves.',
  check:'Ask: "Who flies the plane? Who talks to pilots on the radio? What do we do before take-off?" Pilot + tower + buckle-up = pass.',
  safety:'At airports: hold hands, stay behind lines, belts and trays are for grown-ups to manage. Aeroplanes are flown by trained pilots — never by children, even very enthusiastic ones.'},
 'farmer':{
  title:'Farmer',one:'From seed to supper — how food grows and your child’s first farm job.',
  seoTitle:'Farmer Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Where Food Comes From (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what farmers do through the seasons, how seeds become food, and a grow-a-bean windowsill project.',
  intro:'This lesson follows food from soil to plate and hands your child a real farmer’s job: growing something from seed, day by day.',
  what:'A farmer is a food helper who grows vegetables, fruit and grains, and cares for farm animals.',
  mean:'Farms have fields, greenhouses, barns and tractors. Farmers plant, water, weed, harvest, feed animals and carry the food to markets and shops for all of us.',
  know:'Food knowledge for fours: food grows in soil or on trees and plants, animals give us milk and eggs, and every carrot in the shop was grown by someone.',
  why:'"Where does food come from?" is a four-year-old’s favourite mystery. Knowing the farmer grows brave eating — children try foods they helped imagine from seed.',
  dev:'Patience across days (the farmer’s core lesson), plant-life-cycle sequencing, responsibility routines (daily watering), nature vocabulary, and food-to-farm mapping.',
  vocab:'farmer, seed, harvest, crops, barn, soil',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a farmer. Farmers grow the food we eat and take care of farm animals."\n2) Find the fields, the tractor, the animals. "The tractor helps the farmer work the big fields."\n3) Tell the seed story: seed into soil → rain and sun → roots drink → sprout → grow → harvest → shop → OUR plate. Use your arm as the growing stem.\n4) Match tonight’s dinner to farms: "This potato? A farmer dug it up. This apple? Picked from a tree."\n5) Start the windowsill farm today — the lesson is not finished until something sprouts.',
  say:'"Farmers grow our food. A carrot starts as a tiny seed — the farmer waters it and waits, and waits, until one day it is ready. Farmers are patient helpers."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the farmer holding? Which animals can you find? Where do you think the food goes after the farm? Is it planting time or harvest time on this card?',
  ask:'"Where do carrots come from?" "What does the farmer plant?" "Who gives us milk and eggs?" "What are YOU growing on our windowsill?"',
  childSay:'The farmer’s patience line: "Seeds need water, sun and time." And the dinner recap: "The farmer grew this, the shop sold it, we cooked it!"',
  real:'Farmers’ markets let your child meet real growers — ask one question ("what is ripe now?"). Farm-visit days in autumn are worth the trip: pumpkins, tractors, mud.',
  play:'Windowsill farm: beans on wet cotton in a jar, or herb seeds in a cup. Your child is the farmer: water daily, check leaves, measure with a ribbon. First sprout = celebration; first wilt = farmer problem-solving.',
  tips:'Pick the three foods your child actually eats and trace each to its farm — relevance beats completeness. Keep the watering job real: a plant that depends on them is responsibility with leaves.',
  check:'Ask: "Who grows our food? What does a seed need to grow? What did the farmer harvest?" Food + water-sun-time + naming a crop = pass.',
  safety:'Farms are workplaces: tractors, tools and animals are the farmer’s, not toys. Garden berries and mushrooms are never eaten without a grown-up’s yes.'},
 'chef':{
  title:'Chef',one:'Behind the kitchen door — how restaurant meals happen and your sous-chef job.',
  seoTitle:'Chef Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Kitchen Learning (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what chefs do in restaurant kitchens, the order-cook-serve sequence, safe kitchen jobs and a menu game.',
  intro:'This lesson opens the restaurant kitchen door: what chefs do, how an order becomes a meal, and which kitchen jobs belong to little hands.',
  what:'A chef is a food helper who cooks meals in a restaurant kitchen for many people at once.',
  mean:'Restaurant kitchens are busy teams: orders come in, chefs chop, cook, taste and plate, and servers carry plates out. Chefs wear aprons; many wear tall hats.',
  know:'Kitchen know-how for fours: meals are built in steps (choose → cook → plate → serve), tasting and adjusting is part of cooking, and hot things and knives are grown-up tools.',
  why:'A restaurant visit becomes a discovery tour when your child knows the machinery behind the menu. Sous-chef jobs at home also grow brave, curious eaters.',
  dev:'Sequencing (order→cook→plate→serve), measurement and counting in real cooking, sensory vocabulary (sizzle, tangy, fresh), team-role thinking, and brave-tasting pride.',
  vocab:'chef, kitchen, menu, order, ingredient, plate',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a chef. Chefs cook food for many people in a big kitchen."\n2) Find the hat, the pans, the stove. "Everything in a kitchen has a job — just like the chef."\n3) Tell the order’s journey: you choose from the menu → the waiter tells the chef → the chef cooks and plates it → the waiter brings it out. Four beats, hand to hand.\n4) Name the smells and sounds: onions sizzle, garlic smells strong, pans clatter. Kitchens talk to your nose and ears.\n5) Assign a real sous-chef job tonight — the lesson lands in your own kitchen.',
  say:'"In a restaurant you choose your food from the menu, and the chef cooks it for you. Chefs taste as they cook — that is how they know it is just right."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the chef cooking? What can you smell on this card (imagine!)? How many pans or bowls can you count? Is the kitchen calm or busy?',
  ask:'"Who cooks the food in a restaurant? What does the chef do first — chop, cook or plate? Which kitchen jobs are for grown-ups? What would YOU put on a menu?"',
  childSay:'The sous-chef call-out: "Yes, chef!" And the tasting words: "Mmm, needs more…?" — trying a taste and describing it out loud.',
  real:'At a restaurant, watch the kitchen window: orders go in, plates come out. At home, name the chef moments: "this whisking is chef work!"',
  play:'Home restaurant: paper menus with three dishes (drawn or glued pictures), your child takes the order, "cooks" with play food or helps with the real easy dish, plates it with a leaf garnish, and announces: "Table two, ready!" Swap chef and waiter roles.',
  tips:'Give the tasting job before the eating job — chefs taste, and tasting without pressure grows food bravery. One real task (tearing lettuce, stirring) beats an hour of toy cooking.',
  check:'Ask: "Who cooks in a restaurant? What comes first — order or cooking? Which jobs are yours in our kitchen?" Chef + order-first + safe-jobs = pass.',
  safety:'Stoves, ovens and knives are grown-up tools — a child’s kitchen jobs are washing, tearing, stirring and sprinkling. A grown-up stays in the kitchen the whole time.'},
 'baker':{
  title:'Baker',one:'Flour, patience, warm bread — the early-morning magic of the bakery.',
  seoTitle:'Baker Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach How Bread Is Made (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what bakers do before sunrise, how dough rises, and a knead-and-shape bread-roll bake for little hands.',
  intro:'This lesson follows flour to warm bread — measure, knead, wait, bake — and ends with rolls your child shaped themselves.',
  what:'A baker is a bread helper who makes bread, buns and cakes fresh every day, starting long before we wake.',
  mean:'Bakeries have big ovens, floury tables and shelves that fill up by morning. Bakers measure, mix, knead, shape and bake — and yeast makes dough rise like a pillow.',
  know:'Bread know-how: dough needs kneading, resting (yeast works while we wait!) and a hot oven; ovens and hot trays are grown-up territory.',
  why:'Baking is a science experiment you can eat: measuring is maths, rising is biology, kneading is muscle work. And the smell of your own bread is a memory glued for life.',
  dev:'Measurement and counting with purpose, patience in two acts (kneading and rising), sensory language (soft, springy, warm), sequencing, and fine-motor strength.',
  vocab:'baker, dough, knead, yeast, rise, oven',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a baker. Bakers make our bread while the whole street is still asleep."\n2) Find the bread, the oven, the flour. "Flour, water and a little yeast — that is the whole secret."\n3) Tell the dough story: mix → knead (push, fold, push, fold) → wait (the yeast puffs it up!) → shape → bake → smell → eat.\n4) Explain the mystery simply: yeast is a tiny helper that makes bubbles in the dough — that is how soft bread happens.\n5) Bake rolls together this week: your child measures, kneads and shapes; you handle the oven.',
  say:'"The baker mixes flour and water and kneads the dough — push, push, fold, fold. Then we wait while the yeast makes it fluffy. Then it bakes, and the whole house smells like morning."',
  notice:'Ask: what has the baker made so far? Can you find the oven? What shape are the loaves? What time of day do you think it is — and why?',
  ask:'"What does the baker make? What does kneading feel like? Why do we wait before baking? Which part is for grown-ups?"',
  childSay:'The knead chant: "Push, push, fold, fold!" And the waiting line: "The yeast is working — the dough is sleeping and growing."',
  real:'Walk past a bakery before breakfast and just breathe. At the shop, find breads by shape: long, round, braided. Every loaf was shaped by hands.',
  play:'Bake bread rolls: your child measures cups, kneads half the dough, shapes snakes, snails and knots on the tray. Watch the rise through the oven door together, then eat warm with butter. Repeat monthly — the ritual is the curriculum.',
  tips:'The waiting is the hardest and best part — set a timer and check the dough’s growth together at each beep. Flour mess is the tax on wonder; a damp cloth closes the lesson.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the baker make? What makes dough rise? Whose hands shape the rolls?" Bread + yeast-bubbles + your child’s = pass.',
  safety:'Ovens and hot trays are grown-up only. Wash hands before baking; let bread cool before small fingers tear it. No nibbling raw dough with yeast.'},
 'gardener':{
  title:'Gardener',one:'Water, sun and patience — how gardens grow and your adopted plant.',
  seoTitle:'Gardener Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Plant Care (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what gardeners do, what plants need, weeding and watering, and an adopt-a-plant responsibility project.',
  intro:'This lesson walks the gardener’s round — planting, weeding, watering, trimming — and ends with a plant your child adopts and owns.',
  what:'A gardener is a plant helper who takes care of gardens and parks so flowers bloom and trees stay healthy.',
  mean:'Gardeners plant seeds and seedlings, pull weeds (plant robbers!), water, trim hedges, feed the soil with compost and sweep the paths.',
  know:'Plant needs in child-size: water, sunshine, soil and time. And the gardener’s secret: weeds steal the water, so out they come.',
  why:'Gardens run on patience — nothing rushes a seed. A child who waters a plant daily learns responsibility the honest way: something alive depends on them.',
  dev:'Daily-care responsibility, plant-need reasoning, patience across weeks, fine motor (pinching weeds, gentle watering), and nature observation habits.',
  vocab:'gardener, seed, weed, compost, bloom, roots',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a gardener. Gardeners help plants grow big and strong."\n2) Find the plants, the watering can, the tools. "Every tool has a job — watering, digging, trimming."\n3) Name the plant’s needs with your fingers: water (rain fingers), sun (arms up), soil (wiggle toes), time (tap wrist).\n4) Introduce the villain kindly: "Weeds are plant robbers — they drink the water. The gardener pulls them out so flowers can eat."\n5) Adopt a plant today: one pot, one name, one job — your child’s.',
  say:'"The gardener waters the plants and pulls the weeds. Water, sunshine, soil and time — then one day: a flower! Growing things cannot be rushed, and that is the fun."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the gardener doing first? Can you find a flower and a leaf? What does the soil look like — wet or dry? What tool would you pick up first?',
  ask:'"What do plants need to grow? Why does the gardener pull weeds? What is your plant’s name? How will you know it is thirsty?"',
  childSay:'The needs list, counted on fingers: "Water, sun, soil, time." And the care report each morning: "My plant is… (dry/droopy/happy)."',
  real:'Walk the park as gardener-detective: fresh mulch here, trimmed hedge there, new tulips — someone did all of it. Community gardens love little visitors.',
  play:'Adopt-a-plant: one sturdy pot plant (herbs are forgiving), named by your child. Their job: check leaves, test soil with one finger, water gently with a small can. A ribbon measures growth; a calendar tick marks the care.',
  tips:'Choose forgiving plants — mint, geraniums, spider plants. A wilted plant is not a failure, it is a clue: droopy = thirsty, yellow = too much love (water). Gardeners solve problems; so can fours.',
  check:'Ask: "What do plants need? Why do we pull weeds? Whose job is our plant?" Needs + weeds + theirs = pass.',
  safety:'Wash hands after soil play and wear gloves for digging. Some plants — berries, mushrooms, sap — are not for touching or tasting: ask a grown-up first, always.'},
 'veterinarian':{
  title:'Veterinarian',one:'The animal doctor — check-ups for pets and gentle hands for every creature.',
  seoTitle:'Veterinarian Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Animal Care (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what veterinarians do, how animals show feelings, gentle petting rules and a full teddy pet-clinic play.',
  intro:'This lesson opens the animal clinic: how vets examine patients who cannot talk, what animals’ bodies tell us, and how to be gentle with real creatures.',
  what:'A veterinarian is an animal doctor who helps pets and farm animals stay healthy and heal when they are hurt.',
  mean:'Vets work in animal clinics. They examine, vaccinate, treat wounds and teach owners how to care for their animals — from kittens to cows.',
  know:'Animal-care basics for fours: animals cannot say where it hurts, so we watch them carefully; we ask the owner before touching any animal; pets need quiet, food, water and gentle hands.',
  why:'Fours run on animal love. Channelling it through the vet’s watching skills builds real empathy — and the ask-before-petting rule prevents the bites that end friendships with dogs.',
  dev:'Observation skills (body-language reading), gentleness control, asking permission, care routines for living things, and job-vocabulary (clinic, patient, check-up).',
  vocab:'veterinarian, vet, patient, gentle, check-up, paws',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a veterinarian — an animal doctor. When a pet is sick, the vet helps."\n2) Find the animals, the tools, the clinic. "The vet listens to hearts, looks in ears and checks paws — just like your doctor."\n3) Explain the vet’s superpower: "Animals cannot say ‘my tummy hurts’. So vets watch very carefully: how does the animal move? Eat? Sleep?"\n4) Teach the two animal rules: always ask the owner before touching, and be gentle — soft voice, soft hands.\n5) Open the teddy clinic and practise the whole visit.',
  say:'"The veterinarian is an animal doctor. Animals cannot tell us where it hurts, so the vet watches carefully and checks gently. And we always ask before we pet any animal."',
  notice:'Ask: which animals can you find? What is the vet doing — listening, looking, wrapping? What is on the table? Can you find an animal that looks calm?',
  ask:'"Who is an animal doctor? Why do vets watch instead of ask? What do we say before touching a dog? What does a pet need every day?"',
  childSay:'The permission line: "May I pet your dog, please?" And the clinic line: "Let me check you gently — tell me if it hurts."',
  real:'Vet-visit stories from your own pets or friends’ pets. On walks, watch dogs from afar and read tails: wagging loose = happy, stiff and tucked = give space.',
  play:'Teddy pet clinic: booking desk (paper appointments), waiting room (cushions), exam table (couch arm). Your child the vet checks ears, paws and heart (gentle tummy taps), wraps a tissue bandage and gives a "kind words" prescription. You bring in the "worried owner" voice.',
  tips:'Read animal body language together at the park: stiff body, tucked tail, whale-eye — all "space please" signs. Praise asking behaviour more than petting itself.',
  check:'Ask: "Who is an animal doctor? What do we ask before petting? How do gentle vet hands touch?" Vet + ask-first + soft hands = pass.',
  safety:'Always ask the owner before touching any animal, even friendly-looking ones. Never disturb animals while they eat or sleep, and never touch strange cats, dogs or wildlife.'},
 'scientist':{
  title:'Scientist',one:'Look, guess, test, look again — the scientist’s method in your kitchen.',
  seoTitle:'Scientist Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Thinking Like a Scientist (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what scientists do, guess-and-test thinking, a three-jar sound experiment and the words of real discovery.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the scientist’s loop — observe, guess, test, look again — and runs a first experiment with jars and a spoon.',
  what:'A scientist is a discovery helper who asks "why?" and finds answers by testing carefully.',
  mean:'Scientists work in labs, outdoors and in space. They watch closely, make a guess (a hypothesis), run experiments, measure what happens and share results with other scientists.',
  know:'The scientific loop in child words: LOOK, GUESS, TRY, LOOK AGAIN. Guessing is not cheating — it is the first step of every real discovery.',
  why:'The scientist mindset is the gift that keeps giving: "let’s find out" beats "because I said so", and a flopped experiment becomes "interesting!" instead of failure.',
  dev:'Curiosity routines, guess-before-test inhibition (thinking before doing), observation vocabulary, outcome reasoning, and flop-resilience.',
  vocab:'scientist, experiment, guess, observe, discover, result',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a scientist. Scientists find out why things happen by testing them."\n2) Find the tools: what might be in those flasks and papers? "Scientists write everything down."\n3) Teach the loop with your hands: eyes (look), finger to chin (guess), hands (try), eyes again (look again!).\n4) Explain the golden rule: "A guess is a good beginning. Scientists guess FIRST, then test — the test tells the truth."\n5) Run the three-jar experiment now: guess, tap, listen, compare.',
  say:'"A scientist looks, guesses, tries and looks again. If we wonder about something, we can be scientists too: we guess first, then we find out!"',
  notice:'Ask: what is the scientist holding or mixing? What might be inside the jars? Is the scientist writing something down? What colour is the "science" on this card?',
  ask:'"What does a scientist do first — guess or test?" "What are YOU wondering about this week?" "What happened in our jar experiment? What would you try next?"',
  childSay:'The loop chant: "Look, guess, try, look again!" And the flop line, borrowed from every lab: "Interesting! Let’s try it another way."',
  real:'Scientists are everywhere: weather presenters, bakers testing recipes, gardeners testing soil. When anyone says "let’s test it", name it: "that is science!"',
  play:'Three-jar sound test: jars filled full, half and empty of water. Before tapping: everyone guesses which sounds highest. Tap with a spoon, listen, line them up low-to-high. Then the real experiment: can you play a tune?',
  tips:'Write down your child’s guesses like a real lab book — being taken seriously fuels scientists. Say "interesting!" to surprises, including messy ones: data is data.',
  check:'Ask: "What does a scientist do first? What comes after the guess? What did our jars show us?" Look-guess-try + a remembered result = pass.',
  safety:'Kitchen-lab rules: nothing gets tasted, goggles (or swimming glasses) for anything splashy, and a grown-up runs any experiment with heat or liquids.'},
 'dentist':{
  title:'Dentist',one:'Count, clean, keep strong — the dentist visit and the brushing habit.',
  seoTitle:'Dentist Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Tooth Care (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what dentists do at a check-up, honest visit expectations, the brushing routine and a count-your-teeth game.',
  intro:'This lesson makes the dental chair familiar before your child sits in it — what the dentist does, what the tools do, and the brushing routine that keeps visits easy.',
  what:'A dentist is a tooth helper who checks teeth, cleans them, fixes small holes and teaches everyone the best brushing moves.',
  mean:'Dental clinics have a big reclining chair, a bright lamp, a mirror on a stick and tiny scrapers. A dental nurse helps beside the dentist.',
  know:'Tooth know-how: dentists count and clean teeth; check-ups are mostly looking; brushing happens morning and night with a pea-sized dab of paste.',
  why:'Dental fear is learned from adults. An honest, specific story — chair, lamp, mirror, count — plus a solid brushing habit keeps first visits boring, which is exactly right.',
  dev:'Health-routine ownership (the brushing habit), counting practice on a real mouthful, honest-body-talk vocabulary, and calm waiting skills.',
  vocab:'dentist, check-up, clean, brush, toothpaste, cavity',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a dentist. Dentists keep our teeth healthy and strong."\n2) Find the chair, the lamp, the tools. "The chair lies down like a rocket seat, and the lamp is a little sun."\n3) Tell the check-up in order, honestly: lie back → open wide → the dentist counts and looks with a tiny mirror → maybe a clean and polish → done, with a sticker.\n4) Be honest about the odd bits: the mirror is cold, the polish buzzes, holding your mouth open is strange work — and you can always raise a hand to say so.\n5) Brush twice a day together: outside circles, inside, chewing tops — two minutes, song optional but recommended.',
  say:'"The dentist counts your teeth and keeps them strong. We brush morning and night so the dentist finds happy, clean teeth. If anything feels strange in the chair, you tell the dentist — they will stop and explain."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the dentist looking at? Can you find the little mirror? What is the lamp for? Does the patient look calm or worried?',
  ask:'"Who keeps our teeth strong? What does the dentist do first at a check-up? How many teeth can you count in the mirror? When do we brush?"',
  childSay:'The open-wide line: "Ahh!" with five seconds of stillness. And the honest line: "That feels cold/funny" — practised on a teddy first.',
  real:'Count teeth in the mirror after brushing — most fours have twenty. Spot other people brushing in books and films; dentist talk stays light and daily, not pre-visit only.',
  play:'Teddy dental clinic: your child the dentist, toothbrush for a teddy with big paper teeth. Count aloud ("one, two… twenty!"), brush the sugar bugs away, then swap — you brush your child’s real teeth while they do the dentist-voice. Two minutes, twice a day.',
  tips:'Brush FOR them until about six — their technique cannot yet do the job alone; let them "finish" for pride. Book check-ups for mornings when possible; tired teeth-jaws clamp.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the dentist do at a check-up? When do we brush? What do we say if something feels strange in the chair?" Count + twice-a-day + tell-the-dentist = pass.',
  safety:'Pea-sized toothpaste only, and teach spitting — toothpaste is for brushing, not swallowing. Only grown-ups floss little teeth.'},
 'optometrist':{
  title:'Optometrist',one:'How eyes are checked and glasses are chosen — the eye-test game.',
  seoTitle:'Optometrist Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Eye Health (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what optometrists do, what happens at an eye test, glasses as helpers, and a cover-the-eye shape game.',
  intro:'This lesson normalises eye tests before the first one: what the optometrist does, why some eyes wear glasses, and how the test works as a game.',
  what:'An optometrist is an eye helper who checks how well eyes see and finds glasses when eyes need extra help.',
  mean:'Eye clinics have a big chair, a chart of letters and pictures far away, a little light to look into eyes, and drawers of trial glasses to try.',
  know:'Eye know-how: eye tests are games of naming what you see; some eyes need glasses to see clearly; glasses are helpers, like helmets for vision.',
  why:'Children with blurry vision often do not know — blur is their normal. Making eye tests ordinary and game-shaped means early help instead of years of squinting.',
  dev:'Self-report skills ("it is clearer now!"), one-eye-at-a-time control, naming-and-matching practice, and the normalisation reflex: glasses = ordinary as socks.',
  vocab:'optometrist, eyes, glasses, blurry, clear, eye chart',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is an optometrist. They check eyes so everything looks clear and easy to see."\n2) Find the chart, the glasses, the chair. "Some pictures on the chart are big and some are tiny — the optometrist asks what you can see."\n3) Explain glasses kindly: "Sometimes the eye’s window needs a little help. Glasses are that help — like goggles for seeing."\n4) Play the eye-test game: cover one eye, name shapes far away, swap eyes. Both eyes get a turn.\n5) If a real test is booked, tell the story in advance: chair, chart, "which one is clearer, one or two?"',
  say:'"The optometrist checks how your eyes see. You look at pictures far away and say what they are. If your eyes want extra help, you get glasses — and then the world looks sharp."',
  notice:'Ask: what is on the eye chart? What is the optometrist holding near the eyes? Can you find glasses on this card? Who is wearing them?',
  ask:'"Who checks our eyes? What do we do at an eye test? What do glasses do? Who do you know who wears glasses?"',
  childSay:'The test lines: "I can see a boat/star/apple!" And the honesty line that matters most: "This one is clearer!" — eyes get a vote.',
  real:'Glasses-spotting on every outing: the baker, the storybook bear, Grandpa. The more ordinary glasses look, the happier any future glasses day will be.',
  play:'Wall chart game: stick shapes and letters at kid height, step back three steps, name them — one eye, then the other, then both. Your child "tests" you next. (Play, not diagnosis — real checks belong to the optometrist.)',
  tips:'Watch for squinting, head-tilting, sitting very close to screens or books, and "my eyes are tired" — all gentle reasons to book a real check. Frame any first visit as a game day.',
  check:'Ask: "Who checks our eyes? What do we say during the test? What do glasses do?" Optometrist + say-what-you-see + helpers = pass.'},
 'sanitation-worker':{
  title:'Sanitation Worker',one:'Where rubbish really goes — bins, trucks and the sorting game.',
  seoTitle:'Sanitation Worker Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Recycling (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what sanitation workers do, the journey of rubbish and recycling, truck safety and a three-bin sorting game.',
  intro:'This lesson follows the bin truck’s route: who empties our bins, where "away" actually is, and how sorting recyclables becomes a daily family game.',
  what:'A sanitation worker is a cleaning helper who collects our bins and waste so the neighborhood stays clean and healthy.',
  mean:'Sanitation workers drive big collection trucks along a route, lifting and emptying bins — some trucks have robot arms! They collect recycling separately so it can be made into new things.',
  know:'Waste know-how for fours: rubbish does not vanish — helpers carry it away; recycling goes in its own bin; trucks are big, loud and cannot see small people.',
  why:"Every child adores bin day. Naming the workers and the route turns adoration into gratitude and habits — and 'throw it away' becomes 'put it in the bin for the helpers'.",
  dev:'Sorting and classifying (the recycling game is real cognitive work), calendar sense (which day is bin day?), gratitude for invisible work, and truck-safety habits.',
  vocab:'sanitation worker, rubbish, recycling, bin, route, collect',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a sanitation worker. They take our rubbish away and keep our street clean."\n2) Find the truck, the bins, the lifting arms. "The truck is strong enough to lift our whole bin — look!"\n3) Follow the journey: bin by our gate → truck lifts and tips → the rubbish rides away → recycling gets sorted and made NEW.\n4) Introduce the sorting idea: paper, plastic, everything else — three bins, three teams.\n5) Learn bin day: mark it on the calendar, put the bin out together the night before.',
  say:'"The sanitation worker keeps our street clean by taking the rubbish away. Recycling goes in its own bin so it can become something new — and on bin day we watch from far back and wave."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the truck lifting? Can you find the driver and the helper? Which bin is for recycling? What colours do you see on the truck?',
  ask:'"Who empties our bins? Where does rubbish go after the truck? What goes in the recycling bin? What day is bin day?"',
  childSay:'The sort call-out while playing: "Paper team! Plastic team!" And the bin-day line: "The truck is coming — let’s watch from the window."',
  real:'Bin day from the window (safely far back): lifting, tipping, crushing — theatre! Visit a recycling point and let your child post bottles into the bank; the clunk is deeply satisfying.',
  play:'Three-bin sorting game: clean "waste" from the recycling drawer (paper, card, plastic bottles). Three boxes, relay race, count each team’s score. Wrong bin? No scolding — "rescue it!" is part of the game.',
  tips:'Praise the invisible: "someone emptied six streets before breakfast — that is strong, kind work". Recycling confusion is normal; keep the rule simple and let your council’s guide settle the details.',
  check:'Ask: "Who takes our rubbish away? Where does recycling go? What do we do when the truck comes — run to it or watch from far back?" Workers + recycling + far-back = pass.',
  safety:'Collection trucks are huge with blind spots — watch from the window or far back on the kerb, never near or behind the truck. Wash hands after touching bins.'},
 'hairdresser':{
  title:'Hairdresser',one:'Inside the salon — capes, scissors and the haircut story from start to finish.',
  seoTitle:'Hairdresser Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Salon Confidence (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what hairdressers do, the haircut story step by step, tool safety and a teddy-salon role play for calm haircuts.',
  intro:'This lesson narrates the whole haircut — cape, mist, snip, buzz, mirror spin — so the real salon chair feels like a story your child already knows.',
  what:'A hairdresser is a grooming helper who washes, cuts and styles hair so people leave the salon feeling great.',
  mean:'Salons have special up-down chairs, big mirrors, spray bottles, combs, scissors and clippers. Hairdressers listen first, then cut carefully and sweep up after.',
  know:'Salon know-how: we sit very still while the hairdresser works; tools are sharp or hot and belong to the hairdresser; telling the hairdresser what we want is part of the job.',
  why:'For children who flinch at haircuts, the fear is the unknown: sounds, capes, stillness. Narrating the process turns each strange element into a chapter they can predict.',
  dev:'Sitting-still stamina (a real self-regulation win), process vocabulary, asking-for-what-you-want, sensory prediction (buzz, mist, snip), and body-care identity.',
  vocab:'hairdresser, salon, cape, scissors, style, clippers',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a hairdresser. They cut and style hair so we look and feel great."\n2) Find the scissors, the mirror, the chair. "The chair goes up and down — that is the fun part."\n3) Tell the haircut story in order: choose the style → cape on → mist of water → snip with scissors or buzz with clippers → dry → mirror spin → "all done!"\n4) Be honest about sensations: the cape feels tickly, the spray is cold, the clippers buzz your hand’s favourite song. Naming makes them friendly.\n5) Rehearse with the teddy salon before any real appointment.',
  say:'"The hairdresser asks what we want, puts the cape on, and cuts slowly and carefully. We sit still like statues, watch in the mirror — and then the big spin to see the new hairdo!"',
  notice:'Ask: what is the hairdresser doing? Can you find the mirror and the cape? Is the customer sitting still? What would YOU ask the hairdresser for?',
  ask:'"Who cuts hair? What does the cape do? Why do we sit very still? What would you tell the hairdresser about your haircut?"',
  childSay:'The order line: "A short cut please, with a spiky top!" And the stillness challenge: "Statue sit!" — five seconds, building up.',
  real:'Narrate your own salon visits; point out salons on walks. Home haircuts: name the sound of the scissors and the mist of the bottle as "salon sounds".',
  play:'Teddy salon: doll in the "chair", paper cape with a towel collar, yarn or paper-strip hair to "cut" with play scissors, a comb, the big finish — mirror spin and "ooh la la!" Swap roles; the barber gets a turn in the chair too.',
  tips:'Book morning appointments and say what will happen in order beforehand. For clipper-fear, let them feel the buzz on their own arm first — vibration is friendlier than surprise. Bring the teddy; even superheroes need haircuts.',
  check:'Ask: "Who cuts hair? What do we do in the chair? Whose hands use the scissors?" Hairdresser + statue-still + theirs = pass.',
  safety:'Real scissors, clippers and hot dryers are hairdresser tools only. In the salon, hands stay in laps — no reaching for tools, no grabbing the cape.'},
 'carpenter':{
  title:'Carpenter',one:'Measure twice, cut once — how wood becomes things that last.',
  seoTitle:'Carpenter Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Building with Wood (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what carpenters do, measuring and joining, safe first-tool practice and an offcut building box.',
  intro:'This lesson follows one plank’s journey into furniture — measure, cut, sand, join — and hands your child the carpenter’s golden rule.',
  what:'A carpenter is a building helper who makes things from wood: furniture, doors, shelves and frames.',
  mean:'Carpenters work in workshops and on sites with planks, saws, hammers, screwdrivers, sandpaper and a tape measure. Their rule: measure twice, cut once.',
  know:'Woodworking basics for fours: measure first, cut second; wood can be sanded smooth; real cutting tools are grown-up tools — children measure, hold and sand.',
  why:'Carpentry turns numbers into objects: a length, a half, a corner. Few lessons make maths as holdable as building something that actually stands.',
  dev:'Measurement sense, sequencing (plan→measure→cut→join→sand), precision pride, hand strength and control, plus tool respect that transfers to every workshop.',
  vocab:'carpenter, plank, measure, saw, sand, join',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a carpenter. Carpenters build with wood — chairs, shelves, birdhouses!"\n2) Find the tools and the wood. "That measuring tape tells the carpenter exactly where to cut."\n3) Tell the build story: choose what to make → measure the wood → cut (grown-up) → sand it smooth → join the pieces → stand back and admire.\n4) Teach the golden rule out loud: "Measure twice, cut once — because you cannot un-cut wood!"\n5) Give your child the child-safe jobs on a real build: measuring, holding, sanding.',
  say:'"A carpenter builds with wood. We measure first — twice! — then cut, then sand until it is smooth as a spoon. Real saws are for grown-ups; measuring and sanding are for us."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the carpenter making? Which tools can you name? What does the measuring tape say? What has already been built on this card?',
  ask:'"What does a carpenter build with? Why measure twice? Which tools are for grown-ups only? What could we build together?"',
  childSay:'The rule, chanted: "Measure twice, cut once!" And the builder’s report: "I measured it and held it and sanded it smooth."',
  real:'Furniture safari at home: find three wooden things and guess how they were joined — nails, screws, clever shapes. Woodworking shows and makers’ fairs are field trips.',
  play:'Offcut workshop: a box of smooth wood offcuts, string, and child-safe sandpaper. Your child sands, sorts by size and builds — a boat, a robot, a doll bed — joined with string and big blocks. Real tools appear only with a grown-up: one nail, one hammer, one board, four hands.',
  tips:'The sanding station is the magic — repetitive, sensory, genuinely useful. Keep offcuts in a dedicated box; loose wood is the best open-ended toy in the house.',
  check:'Ask: "What does a carpenter build with? What is the golden rule? Which jobs are yours on a real build?" Wood + measure-twice + sanding = pass.',
  safety:'Saws, drills and nails are grown-up tools. Around any workbench: safety glasses on, hands clear, and a grown-up runs every cut and hammer strike.'},
 'construction-worker':{
  title:'Construction Worker',one:'Foundations, walls, roof — how the big things around us get built in order.',
  seoTitle:'Construction Worker Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach How Things Are Built (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what construction workers do, the build-it-in-order story, machine names and a build-a-house sequencing game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the biggest sequencing story in town — how a hole in the ground becomes a house — and puts your child in the hard hat.',
  what:'A construction worker is a building helper who builds houses, roads, bridges and schools.',
  mean:'Construction sites have cranes, diggers, cement mixers and brick stacks. Workers dig foundations, pour concrete, lay bricks, fit beams and check walls — each trade builds one part.',
  know:'Building know-how: big things are built in ORDER — foundations, walls, roof, inside; every worker wears a hard hat and hi-vis because sites are real workplaces; sites are never playgrounds.',
  why:'Watching a site change week by week is a living sequencing puzzle. Naming the order and the machines feeds the digger-madness phase with real words.',
  dev:'Sequencing (the foundation of maths!), machine vocabulary, cause-effect (cranes lift because…) patience across long builds, and rule-respect for real workplaces.',
  vocab:'construction worker, site, foundation, crane, bricks, hard hat',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a construction worker. They build the houses, roads and bridges we all use."\n2) Find the hard hat and hi-vis vest. "The hat protects the head; the bright vest says HERE I AM to the machines."\n3) Tell the build order with stacked hands: foundations (the hidden feet) → walls (brick by brick) → roof (the umbrella) → inside (doors, paints, floors).\n4) Name two machines and their jobs: the crane lifts, the digger scoops, the mixer spins.\n5) Find a real site on your walk (from the safe side!) and guess today’s chapter: digging? walls? roof?',
  say:'"Construction workers build big things step by step: first the foundations, then the walls, then the roof. The hard hat keeps every builder safe — and building sites are for builders only."',
  notice:'Ask: what is being built on this card? Can you find the crane or the bricks? What is the worker wearing? What do you think gets built first?',
  ask:'"What gets built first — walls or foundations? What does the crane do? Why do builders wear hard hats? What is being built near our house?"',
  childSay:'The build chant: "Foundations, walls, roof!" And the site rule: "Building sites are for builders — we watch from the fence."',
  real:'Walk past sites (safely) weekly and name today’s chapter. Books with cut-away houses show the hidden order — foundations are always the surprise.',
  play:'Build-a-house game: foundations (blocks flat on the rug) → walls (bricks up) → roof (cardboard) → furniture (dolls move in). Do it wrong on purpose — roof first! — and let your child the foreman correct you.',
  tips:'Learn the machines your child loves by their real names — excavator, not "digger-digger". Sequence talk ("what came first?") is quietly training maths-story brains.',
  check:'Ask: "What gets built first? Why do builders wear hats? Can we play on a building site?" Foundations + hard hat + no = pass.',
  safety:'Building sites are never playgrounds — keep out, even when they look empty or quiet. Machines and materials are workers’ only. Hard hats and hi-vis are real safety gear.'},
 'architect':{
  title:'Architect',one:'Draw it first, build it second — designing houses on paper.',
  seoTitle:'Architect Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Design Thinking (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what architects do, drawing plans before building, floor-plan play and the think-draw-build loop.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the architect’s superpower — imagining first, drawing second, building last — and ends with a floor plan of your own home.',
  what:'An architect is a design helper who draws the plans for buildings before the builders build them.',
  mean:'Architects work in studios with rulers, pencils and computers. They talk to the people who will use the building, draw every room, door and window, and make models to test ideas.',
  know:'Design know-how for fours: we THINK, then DRAW, then BUILD; plans show where things go; changing the drawing is easier than changing the building.',
  why:'Architecture links empathy to maths: who lives here? where does the light come from? Draw-first-build-second is also the peaceful sibling of "measure twice, cut once".',
  dev:'Plan-before-do thinking, spatial reasoning (rooms, doors, paths), drawing-to-communicate ideas, perspective-taking (who will use this room?), and revision pride.',
  vocab:'architect, plan, design, floor plan, model, draw',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is an architect. They draw the house first — then the builders build it."\n2) Find the paper, the ruler, the drawings. "Every line on the plan is a wall, a door or a window."\n3) Tell the architect’s loop: listen to what people need → draw the plan → change it and change it → hand it to the builders.\n4) Compare sizes: "On the plan, your bedroom is this big rectangle. A plan is a house map!"\n5) Draw your home’s floor plan together — then build it in blocks.',
  say:'"The architect draws the house before anyone builds it. Draw first, build second! Where will your room go? Where does the sun come in?"',
  notice:'Ask: what is the architect drawing? Can you find a ruler or a plan? What rooms might be in this building? What would YOUR dream house need?',
  ask:'"Who draws houses before they are built? What does a plan show us? What comes first — drawing or building? Where would you put the garden?"',
  childSay:'The loop chant: "Think, draw, build!" And the designer line: "On my plan, this room is the…" — narrating their own drawing.',
  real:'Spot architect work everywhere: your street’s different roofs, the library’s big windows, the park’s curvy paths — every one was drawn first.',
  play:'Floor-plan studio: draw your home as big rectangles (rooms), lines (doors), squares (windows) — no art quality required, ideas only. Then build it in blocks, matching the plan. Add one dream room: a cake room is a valid architectural choice.',
  tips:'Value the drawing over the beauty — architects communicate, they do not decorate. When a block tower falls, ask "what would you change on the plan?" — revision is the real skill.',
  check:'Ask: "Who draws buildings first? What does a plan show? What comes first — think, draw or build?" Architect + house-map + think-draw-build = pass.'},
 'electrician':{
  title:'Electrician',one:'The power helpers — wires, safety rules and where electricity really lives.',
  seoTitle:'Electrician Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Electricity Safety (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what electricians do, the never-touch-sockets rule, battery-vs-plug sorting and honest home-safety scripts.',
  intro:'This lesson names the person who tames electricity and installs the one rule that matters: children never touch plugs, wires or sockets.',
  what:'An electrician is a power helper who works with wires and electricity — installing and fixing the power that lights our homes.',
  mean:'Electricians run wires through walls, fit plugs, switches and lights, and test circuits with meters. They wear special gloves and follow strict safety rules, because electricity is powerful.',
  know:'Electricity rules for fours: sockets, wires and plugs are never touched or poked; water and electricity never mix; every spark, tingle or funny smell gets reported to a grown-up immediately.',
  why:'"But WHY not?" deserves a real answer: electricity travels in wires like water in pipes, it is invisible, and it can hurt — so the electrician is the only one who opens those pipes.',
  dev:'Hazard recognition, rule-with-reasoning (which sticks!), report-reflex training, sorting skills (battery vs plug power), and respect for invisible forces.',
  vocab:'electrician, electricity, wire, plug, socket, safety',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is an electrician. They work with electricity — the power that lights our lamps."\n2) Find the wires and tools. "Electricians wear special gloves and use testers — electricity is powerful, so they are careful."\n3) Explain it simply: electricity flows in wires like water in pipes. The electrician is the only person who may open those pipes.\n4) Install the rule with its reason: we never poke sockets or chew cables — not because we are in trouble, but because electricity BITES.\n5) Do the power hunt: what in this room runs on batteries, and what runs on plugs?',
  say:'"Electricians are the only ones who work with wires and plugs. We never touch sockets or cables — electricity is strong and invisible. If anything sparks or smells funny, we tell a grown-up straight away."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the electrician holding? Can you find wires or a tester? What safety things is the electrician wearing? What might the electrician be fixing?',
  ask:'"Who may fix plugs and wires? Why do we never poke sockets? What runs on batteries in our home? What do we do if a cable looks broken?"',
  childSay:'The rule, in full: "Sockets and wires are never for touching — I tell a grown-up instead." And the report line: "Come look — something is sparking/broken!"',
  real:'When an electrician visits, let your child watch the testing meter from a safe spot. Battery toys vs plug toys sorting happens naturally every playday.',
  play:'Power detective: tour one room and sort everything into "battery team" (torch, remote, toy) and "plug team" (lamp, TV, kettle). Draw both teams. The plug team gets a red paper "grown-ups only" sticker — drawn by your child.',
  tips:'Answer "why?" with the water-in-pipes picture every time — reasoning survives better than prohibition. Never scare with horror stories; a calm, factual "electricity can hurt, so we have rules" is stronger.',
  check:'Ask: "Who may touch wires and plugs? What do we do if a cable is broken? Can electricity and water play together?" Electrician-only + tell-a-grown-up + never = pass.',
  safety:'Never poke sockets, chew cables or put anything into plugs. Water and electricity never mix — no bath toys near plugs. Report every spark, tingle or burnt smell immediately.'},
 'plumber':{
  title:'Plumber',one:'The hidden waterways — pipes under our home and the drip that gets reported.',
  seoTitle:'Plumber Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Water Systems (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what plumbers do, how water travels at home, reporting leaks early and a pool-noodle pipe-building activity.',
  intro:'This lesson uncovers the hidden water roads under your home: who installs them, who fixes them, and why even a tiny drip gets reported.',
  what:'A plumber is a water helper who works with pipes — installing sinks and taps, fixing leaks and keeping water flowing where it should.',
  mean:'Plumbers crawl under sinks and along pipes with wrenches, tape, seals and a plunger. They fit pipes, fix blockages and test that water goes the right way — and only there.',
  know:'Water-system basics: clean water arrives through pipes, used water leaves through other pipes; drips and puddles get reported early; drains are not for poking things into.',
  why:'Plumbing is invisible infrastructure your child uses ten times a day. Naming the system — and the person who fixes it — turns "where does the water GO?" into a story.',
  dev:'Systems thinking (in-pipes, out-pipes), cause-effect reasoning (drip today, puddle tomorrow), reporting responsibility, bath-time engineering play, and home-mapping.',
  vocab:'plumber, pipe, tap, leak, drain, wrench',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a plumber. Plumbers take care of our water pipes."\n2) Find the pipes, the wrench, the tap. "The wrench turns big nuts that hold pipes together."\n3) Follow the water’s journey: pipe brings clean water to the tap → we use it → used water leaves through the drain → more pipes carry it away. Two directions, one system!\n4) Teach the drip rule: "A tiny drip is the pipe saying ‘fix me soon’ — we always tell a grown-up."\n5) Count the taps and pipes in your home; find the stop tap with a grown-up.',
  say:'"The plumber takes care of our water. Clean water arrives in pipes, used water leaves in pipes. If a tap drips or the floor is wet, we tell a grown-up so the plumber can fix it soon."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the plumber fixing? Can you find a wrench or a pipe? Is the water running or dripping? What tools would a plumber carry?',
  ask:'"Who fixes our pipes? Where does water come from — and where does it go? What do we do about a drip? What should never go into a drain?"',
  childSay:'The report line: "Drip drip — come look, the tap needs the plumber!" And the drain rule: "Only water goes down the drain."',
  real:'Under-sink safari (cabinet open, together): find the U-bend and the pipes. Every tap and toilet is plumbing your child already uses — name the system each time.',
  play:'Bath pipe-works: pool-noodle pipes cut in half, funnels, cups and jugs. Build routes from the tap-end to the bucket-end: which path carries the most water? Where does it leak? You are doing real plumbing.',
  tips:'Report drips the same day and let your child hear you phone the plumber — early reporting is the actual lesson. Keep a small "fix-it list" on the fridge; your child can draw the dripping tap as item one.',
  check:'Ask: "Who fixes pipes and leaks? What do we do when we see a drip? Do toys go down the drain?" Plumber + report + never = pass.',
  safety:'Wet floors are slippery — report every puddle, do not splash-test it. Nothing poked into drains or taps, ever; that is the plumber’s job with real tools.'},
 'mechanic':{
  title:'Mechanic',one:'Listen, lift, fix — how cars get their check-ups at the garage.',
  seoTitle:'Mechanic Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach How Cars Are Fixed (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what mechanics do, the car check-up story, tyre and brake talk and a safe tyre-inspection activity.',
  intro:'This lesson demystifies the garage: what mechanics do, how they find what is wrong, and which parts of the car your child can safely inspect.',
  what:'A mechanic is a machine helper who fixes cars — finding what is wrong and making it right again.',
  mean:'Mechanics work in garages with car lifts, ramps, spanners and computers that talk to the car. They change oil and tyres, listen for strange noises and check brakes and lights.',
  know:'Car-care basics: cars need check-ups like people; mechanics LISTEN and test before fixing; the garage is a workshop — waiting area only for children.',
  why:'Cars are mysterious but learnable: noises have meanings, parts have names. A child who has inspected a tyre meets the mechanic as a colleague, not a stranger.',
  dev:'Diagnosis thinking (symptom → cause), part vocabulary, machine empathy ("the car is telling us something"), observation skills, and workshop-safety respect.',
  vocab:'mechanic, garage, engine, tyre, brakes, lift',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a mechanic. Mechanics fix cars so they run safely."\n2) Find the lift, the wheels, the tools. "The lift raises the whole car so the mechanic can see underneath!"\n3) Tell the garage story: the car makes a funny noise → the mechanic listens and tests → finds the problem → fixes it → the car drives home happy. Four beats.\n4) Name the big checks: oil (the car’s juice), tyres (the shoes), brakes (the stoppers), lights (the eyes).\n5) Inspect the family car together — parked, engine off, keys away.',
  say:'"The mechanic listens to the car and finds what is wrong. Cars need check-ups too — oil, tyres and brakes. Mechanics know every sound a car can make."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the mechanic doing — lifting, listening, fixing? Which parts of the car can you see? What tools do you recognise? Is the car up in the air or on the ground?',
  ask:'"Who fixes cars? How does the mechanic find the problem? What do tyres do? Why do cars need check-ups?"',
  childSay:'The garage line: "My car is making a funny noise — please check it!" And the inspection report: "The tyres are fat and strong. The lights work!"',
  real:'Park visits become inspections: fat tyres or flat? Lights on both sides? The bonnet’s warmth after a drive is engine talk waiting to happen.',
  play:'Tyre and light check (parked, engine off, handbrake on): press a tyre wall — firm! Count the lights, find the mirrors, listen to the door thunk. Your child reports like a real mechanic: "All checked, all good!"',
  tips:'Garage visits turn real when your child names three things mechanics check — most mechanics will happily show the lift for an interested four-year-old. Toy car "garages" at home run the same story on small scale.',
  check:'Ask: "Who fixes cars? What do tyres and brakes do? Where do children wait in a garage?" Mechanic + stoppers/goes + waiting area = pass.',
  safety:'Garages are workshops: cars on lifts, tools and fluids are not toys. Children stay in the waiting area — and nobody ever crawls under a car, not even to look.'},
 'bus-mechanic':{
  title:'Bus Mechanic',one:'The depot where fleets sleep — keeping every big bus safe each morning.',
  seoTitle:'Bus Mechanic Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Fleet Safety (Class 33)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what bus mechanics do in the depot, checking brakes and doors on big vehicles, and a depot-inspection game.',
  intro:'This lesson scales up from one car to a whole fleet: the depot where buses sleep, the mechanic who checks every one, and big-vehicle safety rules.',
  what:'A bus mechanic is a big-vehicle helper who keeps a whole fleet of buses safe, checked and ready each morning.',
  mean:'The depot is a giant garage where buses park, get washed, fuelled and fixed. Bus mechanics test brakes, check doors and ramps, change huge tyres and give every bus a safety check before it carries people again.',
  know:'Depot know-how: a fleet means EVERY bus must be safe, not just one; big vehicles have big blind spots; we never climb on, under or behind parked buses.',
  why:'The fleet idea stretches thinking: dozens of buses, every single one, every single day. It is responsibility at scale — and depot pits and lifts are unforgettable machinery.',
  dev:'Scale comparison (car vs bus parts), systematic checking (one bus, then the next), big-vehicle safety habits, fleet-and-route thinking, and inspection vocabulary.',
  vocab:'bus mechanic, depot, fleet, safety check, brakes, inspection',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a bus mechanic. They check every bus so every ride is safe."\n2) Find the big bus, the lift, the tools. "Everything on a bus is bigger — the tyres, the wrenches, the sounds!"\n3) Explain the fleet: "In the depot sleep MANY buses. The mechanic checks them one by one — brakes, doors, lights, ramps — before any bus carries people."\n4) Contrast with the car garage: one car today; a whole fleet every morning. That is a big, important job.\n5) Set the big-vehicle rule: parked buses are sleeping giants — we never climb on, under or behind them.',
  say:'"Bus mechanics check every bus before it drives: big brakes, big tyres, big doors. If every bus passes the check, everyone rides safely. We never climb on parked buses — even sleeping ones."',
  notice:'Ask: what is the mechanic checking? How many buses can you find? What is bigger on a bus than on a car? Where do you think buses sleep at night?',
  ask:'"Who checks the buses? What gets checked — doors, brakes, what else? Why must EVERY bus be checked? Do we climb on parked buses?"',
  childSay:'The depot line: "Every bus checked, every bus safe!" And the big-vehicle rule: "Parked buses are sleeping giants — we keep away."',
  real:'Any bus ride gains a layer: "the mechanic checked these brakes." Depot spotting from the road (far back); some depots run open days — pit views are jaw-droppers.',
  play:'Depot inspection: line up toy buses. Your child the mechanic checks each: brakes (block behind wheels), doors (open-close count), lights (draw them glowing), ramp (fold-out). Stamp a paper "PASSED" per bus — the whole fleet must pass before the route starts.',
  tips:'Scale talk is the gold: "the mechanic’s wrench is THIS long for bus nuts!" Compare car and bus side by side. Fleet-checking games quietly teach systematic, complete work — the scholar’s habit.',
  check:'Ask: "Who keeps buses safe? What does the mechanic check? Do we climb on parked buses?" Bus mechanic + brakes-doors-lights + never = pass.',
  safety:'Depots and parked buses are real workshops with pits and heavy vehicles — never climb on, under or behind any bus. Watch big vehicles from far back: they cannot see small people.'}
};
